<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jamaat al Muslimeen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jamaat al Muslimeen is a Muslim organisation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad-and-tobago">Trinidad and Tobago</a> led for decades by Yasin Abu Bakr. On 27 July 1990 about a hundred of its members stormed the parliament building and the state television station in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-of-spain">Port of Spain</a>, holding Prime Minister A. N. R. Robinson and other ministers hostage for six days. The coup attempt failed, and the hostage takers surrendered after an amnesty whose validity was later disputed in the courts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jamaat-al-muslimeen.jpg" alt="Jamaat al Muslimeen" width="320" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BaronJaguar · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_Trinidad_and_Tobago_(2025).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The movement grew out of a setting of racial division, black racism and supremacy, the illicit drug trade and the ideology of extremist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a>, and out of a militant Islamic discourse which held that liberation, especially for Afro-Trinidadians and Tobagonians, was to be found only within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Its support came from Afro-Trinidadian Muslims rather than from the older community of Indian Muslims. It first drew on the Nation of Islam's vision of black consciousness but moved increasingly towards Salafism, and it received training and support from the Libyan leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muammar-gaddafi">Muammar Gaddafi</a> through his World Islamic Call Society.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the coup attempt of July 1990, insurgents stormed the parliament and took Prime Minister Arthur Napoleon Raymond Robinson and most of his staff hostage, while others stormed a police station; at 6:00 PM Yasin Abu Bakr announced that the government had been overthrown. Chaos and looting broke out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-of-spain">Port of Spain</a>, and 24 people were killed. The group surrendered in exchange for an amnesty. Abu Bakr and 114 followers received presidential pardons that were later retracted, but a court upheld the amnesty agreement despite the argument that it had been obtained under duress, and no member served jail time for the attack.</p><p>Afterwards the group aligned itself publicly with the United National Congress before the general election of 1995 and later with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-national-movement">People's National Movement</a>. Present and past members were connected to or prosecuted for drug and gang killings, rape and kidnappings for ransom. In 2005 the group was suspected of links to bombings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-of-spain">Port of Spain</a>, and a member was arrested in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> for trying to ship 70 assault rifles from Fort Lauderdale. Three members admitted involvement in the kidnapping, rape and murder of the businesswoman Vindra Naipaul-Coolman in 2006.</p><p>In 2014 the independent senator Dana Seetahal, a former prosecutor against the group, was assassinated; the eleven men arrested in 2015 all belonged to a mosque in Carapo affiliated with it. Abu Bakr died at his home on 21 October 2021, aged 80. His successor as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>, Sadiq al Razi, who had taken part in the coup attempt, called in 2024 for conciliation between the group and the rest of the country.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jamaat al Muslimeen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Jamaat al Muslimeen</b> held the prime minister and parliament hostage in a failed coup attempt in 1990.</blockquote><a href="/state/trinidad-and-tobago">Trinidad and Tobago</a></li></ul></section>
